  "content": {
    "id": "p29-q5",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Find the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)$, specifying the domain of the inverse. A sketch may help.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p29-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=9-x^2,\\ x\\geq0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{9-x},\\ x\\leq9$.",
          "worked": "Interchange $x$ and $y$; the original restriction becomes $y\\geq0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=9-y^2\\\\y^2&=9-x\\\\y&=\\sqrt{9-x}\\\\f^{-1}(x)&=\\sqrt{9-x}\\end{align*}$$\nSince $f(x)=9-x^2\\leq9$ on $x\\geq0$, the inverse has domain $x\\leq9$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=x^2,\\ x\\leq0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f^{-1}(x)=-\\sqrt{x},\\ x\\geq0$.",
          "worked": "Interchange $x$ and $y$; the original restriction becomes $y\\leq0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=y^2\\\\y&=-\\sqrt{x}\\\\f^{-1}(x)&=-\\sqrt{x}\\end{align*}$$\nThe original range is $[0,\\infty)$, so the inverse has domain $x\\geq0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=x^2-2x,\\ x\\geq1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f^{-1}(x)=1+\\sqrt{x+1},\\ x\\geq-1$.",
          "worked": "Interchange $x$ and $y$; the original restriction becomes $y\\geq1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=y^2-2y\\\\x+1&=(y-1)^2\\\\y-1&=\\sqrt{x+1}\\\\y&=1+\\sqrt{x+1}\\\\f^{-1}(x)&=1+\\sqrt{x+1}\\end{align*}$$\nSince $f(x)=(x-1)^2-1$ on $x\\geq1$, its range is $[-1,\\infty)$. The inverse therefore has domain $x\\geq-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      }
    ]
  },
